codingChallenge.solutionExample:
// Problem 1: Plaintext password comparison assumes passwords are stored
// in plaintext, which is a severe security failure. Solution: hash
// passwords before storing them, and compare using a hashing library's
// verify function instead of direct equality (covered in lesson 3).

// Problem 2: Forces the client to resend email+password on every single
// request, multiplying exposure risk. Solution: issue a token or session
// after a successful login, and have the client present THAT instead of
// raw credentials on subsequent requests (lessons 4-6).

// Problem 3: A GET request with a meaningful body doesn't follow normal
// REST conventions. Solution: login should be a dedicated POST /login
// route, separate from routes that read already-authenticated data.

sections.content:
Role-based access control: checking whether a user's assigned role permits a given action, typically implemented as middleware (directly extending Module 7, lesson 8's requireAdmin example):

function requireRole(role) {
  return (req, res, next) => {
    if (req.user.role !== role) {
      return res.status(403).json({ success: false, error: 'Insufficient permissions' });
    }
    next();
  };
}

app.delete('/admin/users/:id', requireRole('admin'), deleteUserHandler);

Ownership-based access control: checking whether the authenticated user actually owns the specific resource they're trying to modify:

async function requireOwnership(req, res, next) {
  const post = await postsCollection.findOne({ _id: new ObjectId(req.params.id) });
  if (!post) return res.status(404).json({ success: false, error: 'Post not found' });
  if (post.authorId.toString() !== req.user.id) {
    return res.status(403).json({ success: false, error: 'You do not own this post' });
  }
  next();
}

app.delete('/posts/:id', requireOwnership, deletePostHandler);

Both examples assume req.user already exists, populated by an earlier authentication middleware — exactly the layered middleware composition from Module 7, lesson 8, with authentication and authorization as genuinely separate, sequential stages.

sections.code:
// Authentication middleware: confirms WHO is making the request
// (full implementation in lessons 4-6; simplified here to focus on
// the authorization concept this lesson is teaching)
function requireAuth(req, res, next) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization;
  if (!token) {
    return res.status(401).json({ success: false, error: 'Authentication required' });
  }
  req.user = { id: 'user123', role: 'editor' }; // would be derived from a real verified token
  next();
}

// Authorization middleware: confirms WHAT this now-known user can do
function requireOwnership(req, res, next) {
  const post = posts.find((p) => p.id === req.params.id);
  if (!post) return res.status(404).json({ success: false, error: 'Post not found' });
  if (post.authorId !== req.user.id) {
    return res.status(403).json({ success: false, error: 'You do not own this post' });
  }
  req.post = post; // pass the already-found post forward, avoiding a duplicate lookup
  next();
}

app.delete('/posts/:id', requireAuth, requireOwnership, (req, res) => {
  posts = posts.filter((p) => p.id !== req.params.id);
  res.status(204).send();
});

