After installing bcrypt (npm install bcrypt), hashing a password during signup:

import bcrypt from 'bcrypt';

const saltRounds = 10;
const plainPassword = req.body.password;
const hashedPassword = await bcrypt.hash(plainPassword, saltRounds);

// hashedPassword is what gets stored in the database - NEVER plainPassword
await usersCollection.insertOne({ email: req.body.email, password: hashedPassword });

Verifying a password during login:

const user = await usersCollection.findOne({ email: req.body.email });
if (!user) return res.status(401).json({ success: false, error: 'Invalid credentials' });

const isMatch = await bcrypt.compare(req.body.password, user.password);
if (!isMatch) return res.status(401).json({ success: false, error: 'Invalid credentials' });

// If isMatch is true, the submitted password is correct - proceed to
// issue a token or session (lessons 4-5), still never having stored
// or logged the plaintext password anywhere at any point.

bcrypt.compare() handles the entire 'hash the submitted password and compare hashes' process internally — you never manually re-hash and compare yourself.

