After installing jsonwebtoken (npm install jsonwebtoken), issuing a token at the end of a successful login (directly extending lesson 3's login route):

import jwt from 'jsonwebtoken';

const JWT_SECRET = process.env.JWT_SECRET; // never hardcoded - lesson 9 and Module 5's API key lesson both apply here

const token = jwt.sign(
  { userId: user._id, email: user.email },
  JWT_SECRET,
  { expiresIn: '1h' }
);

res.json({ success: true, data: { token } });

Verifying a token on a later, protected request (full protected-route middleware covered in lesson 6):

try {
  const decoded = jwt.verify(token, JWT_SECRET);
  // decoded now contains { userId: ..., email: ..., iat: ..., exp: ... }
} catch (err) {
  // verification failed: token is invalid, tampered with, or expired
}

The client typically stores the issued token (commonly in memory or local storage, with real tradeoffs covered in lesson 9) and includes it on every subsequent request, conventionally in an Authorization header: Authorization: Bearer <token>.

sections.code:
import jwt from 'jsonwebtoken';
import bcrypt from 'bcrypt';

const JWT_SECRET = process.env.JWT_SECRET;

app.post('/login', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const { email, password } = req.body;

    const user = await usersCollection.findOne({ email });
    if (!user) {
      return res.status(401).json({ success: false, error: 'Invalid credentials' });
    }

    const isMatch = await bcrypt.compare(password, user.password);
    if (!isMatch) {
      return res.status(401).json({ success: false, error: 'Invalid credentials' });
    }

    // Credentials verified - issue a signed token as proof, instead of
    // requiring the client to resend credentials on every future request
    const token = jwt.sign(
      { userId: user._id.toString(), email: user.email },
      JWT_SECRET,
      { expiresIn: '1h' }
    );

    res.json({ success: true, data: { token, email: user.email } });
  } catch (err) {
    next(err);
  }
});

